From: lmoresi <louis.moresi@anu.edu.au>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 15:41:26 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] A ribbon whose trace ends on a curved wall is assembled: the
 area check's tolerance is a triangle's

The ribbon's assembly mesh is checked against its CAD area, to 1e-9 of it.
With the trace's top on an annulus's outer wall (a 15-degree dip), the mesher
put that vertex 4.6e-9 from the CAD's, and the areas differed by 2e-8 of the
total. A zone outcropping there was refused ("the ribbon assembly meshed to
area ... against CAD ..."), and a fault there got that message in place of
the split's own boundary refusal. The later collapse of boundary imprints
removes the vertex: the final wall vertices lie on the wall to round-off.

The check guards against a missing or doubled piece, at least a triangle's
area, so it now allows 1e-3 of the smallest triangle (or 1e-9 of the CAD
area, if larger). Measured on 296 ladder assemblies (dips 10-45 degrees,
tops 0-3 cells below the wall): all but this one agree to 8.3e-16; this one
is 1e-4 of its smallest triangle.

test_0906 (tier B): the zone is placed and reaches the wall, the fault gets
the split's boundary refusal (both fail before the change), and the check
accepts 1e-4 of a triangle and refuses an assembly missing one.

Underworld development team with AI support from Claude Code
